    "lawCode": "Amd §33-1301, En Con L",
    "lawSection": "Environmental Conservation Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S5343\n\nSPONSOR: KAMINSKY\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the environmental conservation law, in\nrelation to prohibiting the use of chlorpyrifos; and providing for the\nrepeal of such provisions upon expiration thereof\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThe purpose of this bill is to prohibit the use of a specific pesticide\nwith significant known adverse human health impacts, especially for\nchildren.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nThis bill would amend § 33-1301 of the Environmental Conservation Law to\nprohibit the use of the pesticide chlorpyrifos. Specifically, the bill\namends Section 33-1301 of the Environmental Conservation Law and makes\nit \"unlawful for any person to use chlorpyrifos.\" However, the bill\ninitially bans aerial application of chlorpyrifos, beginning January 1,\n2020. Beginning January 1, 2021, all applications of chlorpyrifos, other\nthan applied to apple tree trunks, are banned. As of December 1, 2021,\nall uses of chlorpyrifos are banned.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nChlorpyrifos is an organophosphate pesticide, first introduced for sale\nin 1965, which works to block the enzyme cholinesterase and impairs the\ncentral nervous system, an impact also shared with the nerve gas Sarin.\nThe use of chlorpyrifos, which has increased as the use of DDT waned,\nhas been consistently controversial. The increasing usage was accompa-\nnied by an increased number of accidental poisonings, peaking in 1996\nwith 7,000 reports to Poison Control Centers. Despite the poisoning\nrisk, and an increasing number of scientific studies suggesting adverse\nhealth impacts, Dow Chemical continued to insist that the pesticide was\ncompletely safe. In 1993, the New York Attorney General charged Dow with\nfalse advertising, a case subsequently settled with Dow's payment of a\n$2 million fine in 2003.  The United States Environmental Protection\nAgency (EPA) prohibited indoor use of chlorpyrifos in 2001.\n\nChlorpyrifos and other organophosphate pesticides have been the subject\nof extensive scientific study. Links have been made between exposure and\nneuropsychological effects including speech difficulties, memory, atten-\ntion and coordination (see for example \"Chronic Exposure to Organophosp-\nhate (OP) Pesticides and Neuropsychological Functioning in Farm Workers:\na Review\" published January 2016 in the International Journal of Occupa-\ntional and Environmental Health).  Adverse impacts on children have also\nbeen identified in numerous scientific studies. In his 2012 study \"A\n\nStrategy for Comparing the Contributions of Environmental Chemicals and\nOther Risk Factors to Neurodevelopment of Children,\" David C.  Bellinger\nestimated that the use of organophosphate pesticides like chlorpyrifos\nhad led to a collective 17 million loss in IQ points among American\nchildren. In his statement regarding the 2003 settlement on the Dow\nChemical false advertising settlement, Dr.  Philip Landrigan, Chair of\nthe Department of Community and Preventative Medicine at Mount Sinai\nMedical Center said: \"Excellent studies conducted by independent scien-\ntists have clearly shown that chlorpyrifos, the active ingredient in\nDursban, is toxic to the human brain and nervous system and is especial-\nly dangerous to the developing brain of infants.\" In addition, a 2014\nChildhood Autism Risks from Genetics and the Environment (CHARGE) study\nperformed by the University of California Davis Medical Investigation of\nNeurodevelopment Disorders (MIND) found that the risk of having a child\nwith autism almost tripled for women exposed to chlorpyrifos in their\nsecond trimester of pregnancy.\n\nThe EPA recognized the findings of the scientific studies and in Novem-\nber 2016 issued a revised human health risk assessment, laying the\ngroundwork for a chlorpyrifos ban. This bill would enact a chlorpyrifos\nban based on the compelling scientific evidence of the harmful health\nimpacts of chlorpyrifos, especially on children.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2018 A10274 passed assembly\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nJanuary 1, 2020 for aerial applications. January 1, 2021 for all uses\nexcept apple trees. December 1, 2021 for all uses.\n",
